ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor  VOTE: yellow
MANDATORY_BASELINES: [{"name": "text-dedup", "why": "Need to verify if other dominant production pipelines (like text-dedup used in bigcode/ROOTS) share the same NFD-only blindspot as Datatrove. If they default to NFKC, the claim reduces to a Datatrove-specific configuration bug.", "repo_or_cite": "https://github.com/ChenghaoMou/text-dedup"}]
STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["Synthetic twin generation: L1 applied NFKC(d) to synthesize duplicates. This guarantees the pairs isolate the compatibility characters, testing an artificial worst-case rather than the natural distribution of real-world duplicates.", "Datatrove as a single point of failure: assuming Datatrove's simplify_text represents all 'standard near-duplicate dedup pipelines' without verifying alternative standard architectures."]
MISSING_METRICS: ["Organic pair prevalence: What percentage of C4 actually contains naturally co-occurring document pairs that differ only by compatibility/casefold equivalence?", "Natural False Negative rate: The FN rate on organic token-identical duplicates found in the wild, not synthetic NFKC-twins."]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Mine a raw corpus (e.g., C4) for naturally co-occurring document pairs that share identical model token sequences but have different raw bytes. Run Datatrove MinHash on these organic pairs. If the FN rate is low (because natural variations include enough matching raw n-grams) or if such pairs are vanishingly rare, the 95.1% synthetic failure rate is practically irrelevant.
RATIONALE: The evaluation generated pairs by defining twin = NFKC(d). While the base text is real, the duplicate pairs are synthetic and precisely constructed to exploit the normalizer gap. This proves the vulnerability is mathematically open, but provides insufficient evidence that such divergent duplicates actually exist organically in the wild. The 95.1% metric is a recall failure on an adversarial set, not the real-world FN rate of naturally occurring duplicates. I hold at yellow until organic co-occurrence is proven.
